  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1... \nAfternoon Update: No changes to the first key message. See\nprevious discussion below for more details. \n\nPrevious Discussion: A 1025 hPa sfc anticyclone continues to \nbuild in over NY and New England this morning with clear skies \nand calm winds. Some patchy radiative mist/fog in the major and \nsheltered valleys will burn off quickly after sunrise. After a \ncool start in the 40s and 50s (it fell to 38F at Old Forge!), \nexpect max temps to be slightly warmer than yesterday with \nshallow mixing depths. Expect highs to be near or slightly below\nnormal in the 70s to lower 80s with mostly sunny conditions due\nto the subsidence from the sfc high. Humidity levels remain \ncomfortable. Tonight, mostly clear skies and light to calm winds\nwill promote ideal radiative cooling conditions with fog in the\nsheltered valleys once again. We went with a MAVMOS/NBM blend \nand trended cooler with min temps over the northern zones with \n40s to lower 50s north of the Capital Region...and mainly lower \nto mid 50s to the south. The upcoming weekend will feature a \nslight moderation and warming trend in temps, as the sfc \nanticylone slowly moves downstream near the Gulf of Maine by \nSunday. An isolated pop-up shower or t-storm is possible near \nthe western Dacks. Otherwise, dewpoints in the 40s to lower 50s \nover the mtns, and mainly the 50s in the valleys will be \ncomfortable with highs near normal in the lower to mid 80s in \nthe valleys/hills and 70s to around 80F over the higher terrain \nwhich is climatologically normal for late July (i.e. normal high\nfor Albany is 84F currently). The fair and dry weather looks to\nend by Mon. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\nBased on latest ensemble forecast guidances, upper level \ntroughing is favored to return across eastern New York and \nwestern New England beginning Sunday night. The upper level \ntrough is favored by the current forecast to continue into the \nmiddle of next week, but confidence is low on when the upper \nlevel trough moves east and how far southeast it moves for the \nmiddle of next week. For Monday, a surface low pressure system \nis favored to move over the Great Lakes region bringing low \nchances (less than 30 percent) for rain showers during the \nmorning hours and increasing to medium for the afternoon (40 to \n60 percent chances). This brings increasing confidence that  \nrain showers during the morning will likely hinder ingredients \nneeded for strong storms during the afternoon for Monday. \nHowever, a different scenario is favored for Tuesday as the \nsurface low pressure system moves eastward over the Northeast \nbringing a cold front Tuesday evening. Depending on the timing \nof the front, how much moisture we have, and enough ingredients \nneeded, thunderstorms are favored to develop Tuesday afternoon \nand evening across eastern New York and western New England\nwhere a few could become on the strong side. Primary concern\nwould be strong winds with any developed thunderstorm. \nConfidence is increasing for widespread rain shower activity and\nscattered thunderstorms for Tuesday afternoon and evening, but \nthe severity of storms is still uncertain due to timing of the \nfront. \n\nLatest National Blend of Model data suggests for 24 hour \nrainfall amounts exceeding 1 inch is between 40 and 50 percent. \nWith the dry conditions across eastern New York and western New \nEngland over the weekend, confidence is medium to high for \nlimited impacts for flooding as most locations will be able to \nhandle the rainfall, but typical nuisance locations that are \nprone to flooding could see impacts due to potential heavy \nrainfall with thunderstorms. Continue to monitor the latest\nforecast as we get closer to early next week.",
